# test-driven-development (Cursor)

Tests are proof — "seems right" is not done.

## Discover the stack first

Before writing the first test, find how *this* repo tests: language/build
system files, checked-in wrappers (`./gradlew`, `make test`) over globally
installed tools, the test framework and how it runs a single test vs the
full suite, existing conventions, and documented commands (README, CI).
Never assume `npm test` by default.

## The cycle

RED — write a test that fails, proving nothing works yet. GREEN — write
the minimum code to pass, no over-engineering. REFACTOR — clean up with
tests still green, running them after every step.

## The Prove-It Pattern (bug fixes)

Don't start by trying to fix a reported bug. Write a test that reproduces
it first (it should fail, confirming the bug), then fix, then the test
passes (proving the fix), then run the full suite for regressions.

## Test pyramid and sizing

Most tests small (single process, no I/O, milliseconds) — pure logic. Some
medium (multi-process, localhost, no external services) — API/component
tests with a test DB. Few large (multi-machine, external services allowed)
— E2E, limited to critical paths. The Beyonce Rule: if you liked it, you
should have put a test on it.

## Writing good tests

- Test state/outcome, not internal interactions (don't assert which
  methods were called).
- DAMP over DRY in tests — each test self-contained and readable even at
  the cost of some duplication.
- Prefer real implementations > fakes > stubs > mocks, in that order; mock
  only when the real thing is slow, non-deterministic, or has
  uncontrollable side effects.
- Arrange-Act-Assert structure.
- One assertion/concept per test.
- Descriptive test names that read like a specification.

## Anti-patterns

Testing implementation details, flaky timing/order-dependent tests,
testing framework/library code instead of your own, snapshot abuse, no
test isolation, mocking everything (passes while production breaks).

## Verification after implementation

Every new behavior has a corresponding test; the full suite passes via the
repo's own command; bug fixes include a reproduction test that failed
before the fix; no tests skipped/disabled; coverage hasn't decreased.
Don't re-run an unchanged test command just for reassurance.
